---
onetaskgraph_template: 1
description: >-
  A plan's own description: its overview, then every plan-level budget answer in full. The
  planner writes it as the plan project's body, and records the same answers as the
  project's `orchestrator.plan-budgets` metadata, which `just check-plan`, `just
  review-plan` and the design-document writer read.
variables:
  # llmlint: ignore-block[contracts_have_one_source_or_a_drift_gate] These descriptions restate the shape `orchestrator/plan_budgets.py` states once, and `tests/test_plan_budgets_template.py` fails the moment the two part: the variables, each list's keys, the vocabularies and every cap are compared with that module's own.
  overview:
    description: >-
      The plan's own prose: what it sets out to do, as a project body says it.
    type: text
    required: true
  sizing:
    description: >-
      The short "What we're sizing for" paragraph a person reads first: the realistic
      usage the plan's budgets hold at, in a sentence or two, at most 600 characters.
    type: text
    required: true
  workload:
    description: >-
      The full realistic workload the plan's budgets hold at, with numbers: data volume and
      shape, concurrency, items per run.
    type: text
    required: true
  ten_x_summary:
    description: >-
      What the product owner notices getting worse first at 10x realistic usage, on one
      line of at most 240 characters.
    type: text
    required: true
  ten_x:
    description: >-
      The full answer to "at 10x realistic usage, what does the product owner notice
      getting worse first?", naming the `id` of the budget that covers it or saying why
      none does. That budget is an outcome at the level the product owner tracks, such as
      a whole run's quota headroom or a latency someone waits through, never one step's
      share of it, which is telemetry its budget reports as its breakdown.
    type: text
    required: true
  checklist:
    description: >-
      One object per feature concern with `in_scope` (boolean) and four string keys: `concern`
      (the concern, such as latency), `budget` (the `id` of the budget, on one
      of the plan's tasks, that covers it at the level the product owner tracks; its
      per-step figures and second units are that budget's breakdown, not budgets),
      `not_applicable` (a full "n/a because ...") and `summary` (that reason on one line of at most 160 characters). Exactly one of `budget`
      and `not_applicable` is non-empty, the other `""`, and `summary` is non-empty exactly
      when `not_applicable` is. Covered concerns have in_scope=true and reference an owned
      budget. in_scope=false means outside these changes; true with n/a is deliberately
      unbudgeted within these changes. Gate time and change cycle time belong only in
      repo_wide_effects, governed by existing root budgets.
    type: list
    items: object
    required: true
  repo_wide_effects:
    description: >-
      Only actual expected effects on existing root budgets of changed repositories,
      or `[]`. Exactly four string keys: `repository` (normalized origin), `budget`
      (nonempty root-budget id), `effect` (nonempty, never `none`) and `summary`
      (one line of at most 160 characters). Each repository/budget pair occurs once;
      consolidate effects. Verify root-budget existence from the repository read.
      Feature registration and unaffected repositories do not belong here. Legacy
      records retain their historical rules.
    type: list
    items: object
    required: true
  realistic_data:
    description: >-
      One object per kind of data the plan makes realistic, or `[]`, with five string keys:
      `data` (what the data is), `choice` (`generator`, `fixture`, `hybrid` or
      `real-sample`), `reason` (why, weighed on realism, generation cost, fixture cost and
      upkeep), `artifact` (the repository artifact spikes, checks and later plans share) and
      `summary` (the choice and why, on one line of at most 160 characters).
    type: list
    items: object
    default: []
  spike_findings:
    description: >-
      One object per spike finding that changed the plan, or `[]`, with four string keys:
      `spike` (the spike's id), `finding` (what it found), `changed` (what in the plan it
      changed) and `summary` (the finding and what it changed, on one line of at most 160
      characters).
    type: list
    items: object
    default: []
  schema_version:
    description: >-
      Integer literal 2 for newly authored answers. Omitted only for exact legacy records.
    type: integer
    required: false
  # llmlint: ignore-end[contracts_have_one_source_or_a_drift_gate]
---
{#-
  THE READERS. A person opening the plan, the plan review and the plan check. The project's
  `orchestrator.plan-budgets` metadata holds these same answers, and the plan check holds
  this body to being their rendering; the design document restates only each answer's
  summary and links each budget to the task that owns it.

  THIS TEMPLATE. Guidance is always a Jinja comment, never an HTML comment, so a rendered
  description carries none of it. `docs/budgets.md` states the principles the answers are
  held to.
-#}
{{ overview }}

## Budgets

**What we're sizing for.** {{ sizing }}

### Workload

{{ workload }}

### At 10x realistic usage

**{{ ten_x_summary }}**

{{ ten_x }}

### Checklist

{% for entry in checklist %}
{% if entry.budget %}
- **{{ entry.concern }}:** covered by `{{ entry.budget }}`.
{% else %}
- **{{ entry.concern }}:** not budgeted. {{ entry.not_applicable }}
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}

### Repo-wide effects

{% for entry in repo_wide_effects %}
- **`{{ entry.repository }}`**{% if entry.budget %} (`{{ entry.budget }}`){% endif %}: {{ entry.effect }}
{% endfor %}

### Realistic data

{% for entry in realistic_data %}
- **{{ entry.data }}**, {{ entry.choice }}: {{ entry.reason }} Artifact: {{ entry.artifact }}
{% else %}
This plan makes no data realistic.
{% endfor %}

### Spike findings

{% for entry in spike_findings %}
- **{{ entry.spike }}:** {{ entry.finding }} Changed: {{ entry.changed }}
{% else %}
No spike finding changed this plan.
{% endfor %}
